Fix a memory leak in readChar() on a raw vector or binary connection
when the requested bytes contain a nul, so the io target does not
accumulate leaked read buffers.

rawFixedString() (src/main/connections.c) backs readChar() with
useBytes = TRUE, or in a non-UTF-8 locale.  It copies the requested
bytes into an R_chk_calloc() buffer and hands them to mkCharLenCE(),
which signals "embedded nul in string" when they contain a nul.  That
error unwinds past the R_Free(), leaking the buffer on every call:

    readChar(as.raw(0x00), 1L, useBytes = TRUE)

    ==1691==ERROR: LeakSanitizer: detected memory leaks
    Direct leak of 2 byte(s) in 1 object(s) allocated from:
        #0 calloc
        #1 R_chk_calloc    src/main/memory.c:3550
        #2 rawFixedString  src/main/connections.c:5276
        #3 do_readchar     src/main/connections.c:5347
        #4 bcEval_loop     src/main/eval.c:8150

Take the buffer from R_alloc() instead, under the vmaxget()/vmaxset()
bracket the function already sets up for its UTF-8 branch (which uses
R_alloc() for the same reason); memory from R_alloc() is reclaimed by
the error unwind, so nothing is leaked whichever way the function
leaves.

Found by OSS-Fuzz (libFuzzer_r_io, testcase 4610937584418816: a
single 0x00 byte read with readChar(x, c(1L, 5L, 16L, 64L),
useBytes = TRUE)).  Reproduced locally with the io harness; the leak
is gone with this patch.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #332, filed 2026-09-06 (from a
static scan for resources released only on the normal return path);
not yet on R Bugzilla.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

diff --git a/src/main/connections.c b/src/main/connections.c
index 1e60538474..a3333aa3a3 100644
--- a/src/main/connections.c
+++ b/src/main/connections.c
@@ -5259,13 +5259,14 @@ rawFixedString(Rbyte *bytes, int len, int nbytes, int *np, int useBytes)
 	*p = '\0';
 	res = mkCharLenCE(buf, clen, CE_NATIVE);
     } else {
-	/* no terminator */
-	buf = R_chk_calloc(len + 1, 1);
+	/* no terminator; R_alloc, not R_chk_calloc: mkCharLenCE signals on an
+	   embedded nul (see the note above), which would leak the buffer.
+	   vmax is already bracketed above. */
+	buf = R_alloc((R_SIZE_T)len + 1, sizeof(char));
 	if (len)
 	    memcpy(buf, bytes + (*np), len);
 	*np += len;
 	res = mkCharLenCE(buf, len, CE_NATIVE);
-	R_Free(buf);
     }
     vmaxset(vmax);
     return res;
